.TH "tangsible-role" "1" "2026-09-05" "Tangsible" "Tangsible Manual"
.PP
.SH NAME
.PP
tangsible-role - run a single role in isolation
.PP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\fBtangsible role\fR \fIrole_name\fR [\fIansible-playbook args\fR .\&.\&.\&]
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Testing a single role normally means hand-writing a temporary playbook
around it first.\& \fBrole\fR does that for you: it generates a minimal stub
playbook (\fBhosts: all\fR, \fBroles: [\fR\fIrole_name\fR\fB]\fR) in the current directory,
runs it exactly as \fBtangsible-run\fR(1) would, and removes the stub again once
Tangsible exits.\&
.PP
The stub always targets \fBhosts: all\fR; narrow the actual target the normal
way, with \fB-i\fR/\fB-l\fR passthrough arguments.\& If a \fB.\&/roles/\fR\fIrole_name\fR
directory isn'\&t found relative to the current directory, a warning is
printed up front — the run proceeds regardless (Ansible may still resolve
the role from \fB$ANSIBLE_ROLES_PATH\fR, a configured roles path, or a
collection), but the drill-down view'\&s Task definition section won'\&t be
available for it, since the source lookup only ever walks the stub'\&s own
directory tree.\&
.PP
Everything else — the live tree, filtering, the rerun dialog, the diff
picker — works exactly as in \fBtangsible-run\fR(1).\&
.PP
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
Same as \fBtangsible-run\fR(1); see \fBtangsible\fR(1).\&
.PP
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBtangsible\fR(1), \fBtangsible-run\fR(1), \fBtangsible-rerun\fR(1)
